cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "DOUBLE JEOPARDY: The Vulnerability Spiral of Compound Climate Risk Across 5 Islands"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/DOUBLE-JEOPARDY"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/DOUBLE-JEOPARDY"
abstract: >-
  This geospatial study measures two threats to low islands together, sea-level
  rise and the weakening of the reefs and mangroves that buffer it, across five
  island groups (Maldives, Lakshadweep, Seychelles, Fiji, Canary Islands). On a
  bare-earth elevation model (DeltaDTM v1.1), 11.8% of people in the Maldives and
  6.4% in Lakshadweep live at or below 1 m, against 8.9% and 0.8% on the
  Copernicus GLO-30 surface model; the choice of elevation model and of exposure
  measure (people or named places) moves the results more than any other step.
  Mean Degree Heating Weeks over mapped reef rose between 1996-2000 and 2016-2020
  on every reef island, most in Seychelles, but no monotonic trend is significant
  once serial correlation is accounted for. Mangrove extent shows no decline. A
  compound score of exposure and reef heat stress ranks Seychelles and the
  Maldives in a near tie, with the top island changing at an exposure weight of
  0.52. Protection of the 10 km coastal zone does not follow this risk across the
  four island groups with a WDPA record (r = -0.10, n = 4; descriptive only).
  Across about 1.35 million building footprints, statutory coastal setbacks
  reach fewer than one in ten buildings at or below 1 m in Seychelles (25 m) and
  Lakshadweep (20 m), the Maldives and Fiji have no setback for inhabited land,
  and the Canary Islands' 100 m rule covers 85%.
keywords:
  - sea-level rise
  - mangroves
  - coral bleaching
  - compound vulnerability
  - small island developing states
  - digital terrain model
  - protected-area effectiveness
  - remote sensing
  - climate adaptation
  - geospatial analysis
  - coastal setbacks
  - comparative environmental policy
